window.docContents[40].resolve({"/Typeclasses/#Logical-Foundations--Typeclasses--Maps--Identifiers":{"contents":"To define maps, we first need a type for the keys that we will use to index into our maps. Instead of using concrete types, we will use a type variables\n\nuniverse u v\nvariable {α : Type u} {β : Type v} [BEq α] [ReflBEq α] [LawfulBEq α]\n\n\nwhere α is the type of our map keys and β the corresponding values. Looking at ReflBEq and LawfulBEq, we see that these typeclasses:\n\n/-- `ReflBEq α` says that the `BEq` implementation is reflexive. -/\nclass ReflBEq (α) [BEq α] : Prop where\n  /-- `==` is reflexive, that is, `(a == a) = true`. -/\n  protected rfl {a : α} : a == a\n\n/--\nA Boolean equality test coincides with propositional equality.\n\nIn other words:\n * `a == b` implies `a = b`.\n * `a == a` is true.\n-/\nclass LawfulBEq (α : Type u) [BEq α] : Prop extends ReflBEq α where\n  /-- If `a == b` evaluates to `true`, then `a` and `b` are equal in the logic. -/\n  eq_of_beq : {a b : α} → a == b → a = b\n\n\nprovide the assumption that we have an boolean equality (==) on our keys that is reflexive and coincides with proposition equality =.\n\n","context":"Logical Foundations\u0009Typeclasses\u0009Maps","header":"8.4.1. Identifiers","id":"/Typeclasses/#Logical-Foundations--Typeclasses--Maps--Identifiers"}});